===== /candidate/PROOF.md
     1	VALIDATED
     2	
     3	# What is proven
     4	
     5	Under the supplied MPY semantics, `numerical_letter_grade` is partially
     6	correct for every arbitrary finite list whose elements are MPY `Int` or
     7	`Float` values. There is no bound on list length and no finite unrolling in
     8	the target theorem.
     9	
    10	For an input sequence `VS`, the function returns a fresh list containing one
    11	letter-grade string per input, in the same order. Each output is selected by
    12	the table encoded by `gradeValue`: equality with `4.0` gives `A+`; otherwise
    13	the strict descending cutoffs give `A` through `D-`; otherwise the result is
    14	`E`.
    15	
    16	The proof executes the translated function body, including module loading,
    17	name lookup, call-frame creation, parameter binding, list allocation, every
    18	comparison and branch, `append`, return lookup, frame pop, and restoration of
    19	the caller state. It does not replace the function or loop with an
    20	operational summary.
    21	
    22	This is a reachability/partial-correctness result. It is not a separate
    23	machine-checked liveness theorem.
    24	
    25	# Formal claim
    26	
    27	`SPEC.entry` starts from the complete initial MPY state and the exact
    28	`GRADE-PROGRAM` term. For symbolic `VS:ValSeq` satisfying
    29	`allGradeNumbers(VS)`, it establishes:
    30	
    31	```k
    32	<k> ... => ref(0) </k>
    33	<heap> .Map => 0 |-> list(gradeAcc(.ValSeq, VS)) </heap>
    34	<heapLoc> 0 => 1 </heapLoc>
    35	<env> 0 => 0 </env>
    36	<scopeLoc> 1 => 1 </scopeLoc>
    37	<stack> .List => .List </stack>
    38	<ret> noRet => noRet </ret>
    39	<exc> NoExc => NoExc </exc>
    40	<exit-code> 0 => 0 </exit-code>
    41	```
    42	
    43	`SPEC.loop-invariant` is the unbounded circularity. At a real `#loop` head,
    44	with accumulated output `ACC` and unprocessed suffix `VS`, it changes the
    45	output heap value from `list(ACC)` to `list(gradeAcc(ACC, VS))`, preserves the
    46	input and output bindings, and permits only the loop variable's final value
    47	to be existential.
    48	
    49	Its obligations are:
    50	
    51	- Base: `gradeAcc(ACC, .ValSeq) = ACC`.
    52	- Step: one fixed-semantics iteration binds the head, follows one of the
    53	  thirteen actual branches, appends `gradeValue(head)`, and reapplies the
    54	  circularity to the tail.
    55	- Entry discharge: the function initializes `ACC` to `.ValSeq`; the
    56	  circularity yields `gradeAcc(.ValSeq, VS)`, after which the actual return and
    57	  frame-pop rules complete the entry claim.
    58	
    59	# Proof-extension inventory
    60	
    61	## `GRADE-STEP` and `GRADE-PROGRAM`
    62	
    63	- Class: definitional summary (compile-time syntax macros).
    64	- Semantic role: abbreviate the exact AST; no macro remains at runtime and no
    65	  execution step is replaced.
    66	- Domain/context: only the two closed macro terms, with no variables, frames,
    67	  cells, or guards.
    68	- State footprint/value influence: none directly; their expansion supplies
    69	  the program executed by both claims.
    70	- Justification and containment: `kast --expand-macros` produces byte-identical
    71	  KORE for `solution.mpy` and `GRADE-PROGRAM` (`cmp` exit 0).
    72	- Dependents: both target claims.
    73	- Validation: `check_artifacts.py`, the KAST comparison, LLVM execution, and
    74	  the body-mutation probe.
    75	
    76	## `isGradeNumber` and `allGradeNumbers`
    77	
    78	- Class: definitional summaries.
    79	- Semantic role: constrain the dynamic MPY domain; they do not rewrite program
    80	  terms.
    81	- Domain: all `Val` values and all finite `ValSeq` constructor terms.
    82	- Equations: `isGradeNumber(V)` is exactly `isInt(V) orBool isFloat(V)`;
    83	  `allGradeNumbers` has disjoint empty/cons cases and structurally descends.
    84	- State/context footprint: none.
    85	- Value influence: preconditions for both claims and the guarded comparison
    86	  restatements.
    87	- Dependents: `gradeEq`, `gradeGt`, both dispatch rules, and both claims.
    88	- Validation: integer/float mixed smoke tests, arbitrary symbolic `ValSeq`
    89	  proof, and mutation rejection.
    90	
    91	## `gradeEq` and `gradeGt`
    92	
    93	- Class: definitional summaries.
    94	- Semantic role: expose the supplied MPY Int/Float-vs-Float dispatch through a
    95	  dynamic `Val` argument; they do not replace a `<k>` computation.
    96	- Domain: total over `Val × Float`. The `Int`, `Float`, and nonnumeric cases
    97	  are exhaustive and pairwise disjoint.
    98	- Value equations:
    99	  - `gradeEq(I,F) = eqF(intToF(I),F)` and
   100	    `gradeEq(G,F) = G ==Float F`;
   101	  - `gradeGt(I,F) = gtF(intToF(I),F)` and
   102	    `gradeGt(G,F) = gtF(G,F)`;
   103	  - the explicitly nonnumeric totalization is `false`, and is outside every
   104	    comparison-dispatch use.
   105	- State/context footprint: none.
   106	- Value influence: all grading branches and the final result summary.
   107	- Justification: constructor-by-constructor restatement of the corresponding
   108	  fixed rules in `reference-semantics/semantics/float.k`.
   109	- Dependents: `gradeValue`, the dispatch twins, and both claims.
   110	- Validation: exhaustive equation/guard audit, LLVM boundary tests, the
   111	  independently implemented table oracle, false-postcondition rejection, and
   112	  body-mutation rejection.
   113	
   114	## Guarded `applyCmp` simplification rules
   115	
   116	- Class: derived lemmas (the Kit guarded dynamic-dispatch pattern).
   117	- Semantic role: restate existing fixed `applyCmp("==", Int|Float, Float)` and
   118	  `applyCmp(">", Int|Float, Float)` equations over symbolic `V:Val`.
   119	- Complete match domain: any occurrence of the indicated `applyCmp` function
   120	  under `isGradeNumber(V)`. There is no continuation, control-stack, binding,
   121	  or cell wildcard because these are pure function equations.
   122	- Justification scope/containment: `isGradeNumber` is exactly the disjoint
   123	  `Int`/`Float` union, and `gradeEq`/`gradeGt` reproduce the fixed right-hand
   124	  side for each constructor. Thus every match is in one fixed-rule domain.
   125	- State footprint: no cells read, written, preserved, or abstracted.
   126	- Value influence: branch choice and therefore appended strings.
   127	- Dependents: the loop invariant and entry claim.
   128	- Control/value validation: fixed LLVM execution covers every grade outcome;
   129	  the `"Z"` body mutation is rejected; the false output is rejected. There is
   130	  no operational bridge to compare.
   131	
   132	## `gradeValue` and `gradeAcc`
   133	
   134	- Class: definitional summaries.
   135	- Semantic role: name the expected mathematical output without replacing
   136	  execution.
   137	- Domain: `gradeValue` is total on `Val`; target uses are numeric.
   138	  `gradeAcc` has disjoint empty/cons equations and strictly descends through
   139	  its second finite `ValSeq` argument.
   140	- Context/state footprint: none.
   141	- Value influence: they define the result constrained by the invariant and
